<<<
with the title./A * Collection of Poems & Essays by Mary Queen of Scots ’, prefixed by an essay on the character & writings of Mary Siuart./
===
with *the title*./*A* '*Collection of Poems & Essays by Mary Queen of Scots*', prefixed by *an essay on the character & writings of Mary Stuart*./
>>>

<<<
Two notes dealing with recent cases on the subject of company directors are prefixed by the catchwords in very prominent type: * Retirement & Remorse ’./
===
*Two notes dealing with recent cases on the subject of company directors* are prefixed by the catchwords *in very prominent type*: '*Retirement & Remorse*'./
>>>

<<<
The story is prefixed by an introductory sketch of Pope Alexander VIs Spanish ancestry./Every paragraph is prefixed with a kind of title to it.
===
*The story* is prefixed by *an introductory sketch of Pope Alexander VI's Spanish ancestry*./*Every paragraph* is prefixed with *a kind of title to it*.
>>>

<<<
The poor old word preface, with foreword assailing it on one front & prefix on another, is going through troubled times.
===
The poor old word *preface*, with *foreword* assailing it on one front & *prefix* on another, is going through troubled times.
>>>

<<<
pregnant construction. See TECH- NICAL TERMS.
===
**pregnant construction.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
prejudg(e)ment.

Keep the -e- ; see MUTE E.
===
**prejudg(e)ment.** Keep the *-e-*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
prejudice, n. The Committee’s Report adds that without doubt a marked prejudice to the eating of eels exists in Scotland. The prepositions after p. are against & in favour of; this to is transferred from objection; see ANALOGY.
===
**prejudice**, n. *The Committee's Report adds that without doubt a marked* prejudice *to the eating of eels exists in Scotland.* The prepositions after *p.* are *against* & *in favour of*; this *to* is transferred from *objection*; see ^^ANALOGY^^.
>>>

<<<
adv. See QUASI-

preliminary, ADVERBS.
===
**preliminary,** adv. See ^^QUASI-ADVERBS^^.
>>>

<<<
prelude. The noun is pré/lid; the verb used to be prili’d (‘ All the verse quotations & the dictionaries down to c 1830 ’—OED), but is now pronounced like the noun—a remarkable exception to the tendency mentioned in Noun & VERB ACCENT.
===
**prelude.** The noun is prĕ'lūd; the verb used to be prĭlū'd (' All the verse quotations & the dictionaries down to c 1830 '—OED), but is now pronounced like the noun—a remarkable exception to the tendency mentioned in ^^NOUN^^ & ^^VERB ACCENT^^.
>>>

<<<
premature. The pronunciation pré’matur is recommended, but the sound of the e & the place of the accent are both variable; in any case, the last syllable is fully pronounced & not weakened to -cher.
===
**premature.** The pronunciation prĕ'mătūr is recommended, but the sound of the *e* & the place of the accent are both variable; in any case, the last syllable is fully pronounced & not weakened to *-cher*.
>>>

<<<
premier as an adjective is now suggestive
===
**premier** as an adjective is now suggestive
>>>

<<<
The ELEGANT-VARIATIONISt finds it useful (There was a time when the School of Literae Humaniores stood first in point of number, but of late the History School has taken premier place), but would do better to find
===
The ^^ELEGANT-VARIATION^^ist finds it useful (*There was a time when the School of Literae Humaniores stood first in point of number, but of late the History School has taken* premier *place*), but would do better to find
>>>

<<<
find

Q3 some other way out. It is wise to

+ confine
===
find some other way out. It is wise to

+ confine
>>>

<<<
confine it now to such traditional phrases as p. Earl or Baronet (Bacon had no issue of his marriage, the preseni Sir Hickman Bacon, premier Baronet of England, being descended from his half-brother).
===
confine it now to such traditional phrases as *p. Earl* or *Baronet* (*Bacon had no issue of his marriage, the present Sir Hickman Bacon,* premier *Baronet of England, being descended from his half-brother*).
>>>

<<<
première. See FRENCH WORDS.
===
**première.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
premise(s), -ss(es). 1. The noun is pré’mis, the verb primi’z; see Noun & VERB AccENT. 2. The verb is spelt premise, not -ize; see -ISE) (-1zE.
===
**premise(s), -ss(es).** 1. The noun is prĕ'mĭs, the verb prĭmī'z; see ^^NOUN^^ & ^^VERB ACCENT^^. 2. The verb is spelt *premise*, not *-ize*; see ^^-ISE^^) (^^-IZE^^.
>>>

<<<
3. The two noun spellings (-tses & -isses in the plural) may perhaps be thought useful; but ambiguity cannot often arise between the parts of a syllogism (-isses; see, for meaning, TECH- NICAL TERMS) & of a public house (-tses);
===
3. The two noun spellings (*-ises* & *-isses* in the plural) may perhaps be thought useful; but ambiguity cannot often arise between the parts of a syllogism (*-isses*; see, for meaning, ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^) & of a public house (*-ises*);
>>>

<<<
The uniform spelling premise (pl. premises) is recommended. 4. The verb makes -sable; see MUTE E.
===
The uniform spelling *premise* (pl. *premises*) is recommended. 4. The verb makes *-sable*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
being ‘the previously stated’, & the parts of a public house &c. being ‘the aforesaid ’, (facts, places, &c.).
===
being ' the previously stated ', & the parts of a public house &c. being ' the aforesaid ', (facts, places, &c.).
>>>

